  "annotation": {
    "version": 1,
    "task": "Run reviewers like the user's own agent sessions by default (no sandbox, their normal settings and permissions), keeping the sandbox as an option for untrusted code; works toward native Windows support",
    "summary": "Each agent gets a direct run, now the default: in its disposable clone, with the user's own settings plus extra arguments from gud.<agent>.args (such as a permission mode). --sandbox (or gud.sandbox) keeps the existing confined runs. Instead of deleting all agent configuration, a direct review restores the files the change edited to their pre-change versions and removes ones it added, so the reviewer gets the project's usual instructions but not the change's. Commands are found and launched the Windows way (PATHEXT, .cmd through the shell), untested on Windows.",
    "groups": [
      {
        "id": "direct-runs",
        "intent": "A direct run for each agent: the clone, the user's settings, gud.<agent>.args",
        "necessity": "required",
        "serves": "task",
        "removal_impact": "every review would stay sandboxed, more restricted than the user's own agent sessions, and impossible on native Windows",
        "evidence": "Live, on the planted-bug fixture: a direct Claude review with no extra arguments was refused 'node' (requires approval) and could only read the code; with gud.claude.args = '--permission-mode auto' it ran the code, verified the floor bug and found a second verified one in 14 s.",
        "hunks": [
          "h4",
          "h5:1-33,37-38",
          "h6:1-11,14-17",
          "h7",
          "h8",
          "h9",
          "h10",
          "h11",
          "h12:1,7"
        ],
        "notes": "Direct runs start in the clone so the project's own agent settings and instructions load. Each gets the scratch directory with --add-dir, since scratch tests live beside the clone (a stricter permission mode or Codex's workspace-write sandbox would otherwise refuse writes there). Codex and Cursor direct runs weren't run live; Codex isn't logged in here. RunSpec gains cwd, since the two kinds of run start in different places. gud.<agent>.args is read by directArgs in agents.ts, used by both review and `git gud agents`. The browser guidance (CHECK_PAGES) is shared by the direct and sandboxed prompts. The direct prompt says the user's real repository is out of bounds, since nothing stops a direct reviewer from reaching it."
      },
      {
        "id": "restore-config",
        "intent": "Direct reviews undo the change's edits to agent config instead of deleting it all",
        "necessity": "required",
        "serves": "direct-runs",
        "removal_impact": "a direct review would either lose the project's usual agent instructions (how to run the tests) or follow instructions the reviewed change wrote for its reviewer",
        "evidence": "Test: an edited CLAUDE.md is restored to its pre-change text, an untouched AGENTS.md stays, a pkg/AGENTS.md the change added is removed, and the change's code is checked out; the test also checks the restoration shows in git diff and nothing is staged.",
        "hunks": [
          "h20",
          "h21",
          "h22",
          "h23"
        ],
        "notes": "One git diff --name-status against the base (a range's base, the target's parent, or git's empty tree for a first commit, which makes every file 'added'): added agent config is removed, anything else is restored to the base's version. Restored files are written to the working tree only (git show), not staged, so the reviewer's plain git diff shows the restoration; the bugs review found that git checkout staged it, hiding it from git diff. Files the restored config pulls in (an @import, a hook script, an MCP server in the repo) keep the change's version; the README says so. Sandboxed reviews still remove all agent configuration."
      },
      {
